<section #sec:appendix-meta | Document Metadata>

<sub-section | General Usage>

The `<meta>` block holds the document's front matter — its class, title, authors, and abstract — at the top of the file. Its `type` selects the document class.

<code #code:meta-basic>
<meta type=article>
<title | On the Spectra of Linear Operators>
<subtitle | A Survey>
<author>
<name | Ada Lovelace>
<affiliation | Analytical Society>
<email | ada@example.org>
</author>
<date | 2025-06-01>
<abstract | We survey the eigenstructure of bounded operators.>
<keywords | eigenvalues, operators, spectra>
</meta>
</code>

<minipage #mp:meta-basic>
<meta type=article>
<title | On the Spectra of Linear Operators>
<subtitle | A Survey>
<author>
<name | Ada Lovelace>
<affiliation | Analytical Society>
<email | ada@example.org>
</author>
<date | 2025-06-01>
<abstract | We survey the eigenstructure of bounded operators.>
<keywords | eigenvalues, operators, spectra>
</meta>
</minipage>

<sub-section | Fields>

<table csv -numbered>
type, article | book | website
title / subtitle, the document's heading and tagline
author (repeatable), a name with optional affiliation / email / orcid
date, the publication date
abstract, a summary paragraph
keywords, index terms
doi / license / lang, identifiers and rights metadata
</table>
</section>

<section #sec:appendix-config | Configuration>

<sub-section | General Usage>

`<config>` sets document-wide options. It is self-closing — note the trailing slash — and each entry is a flag or a `key=value`.

<code #code:config-basic>
<config number-sections />
<config toc />
<config note-position=margin />
</code>

<sub-section | Options>

<table csv -numbered>
number-sections / number-figures / number-tables / …, turn on numbering for each kind of element
number-depth, how many heading levels carry numbers
toc, generate a table of contents
toc-depth / toc-title, the contents depth and its heading
note-position, bottom (default) or margin for sidenotes
citation-style, the bibliography style
strict-mode, on (default) | literal | strict
theme, the visual theme
sidebar / on-this-page, website navigation chrome
</table>

<sub-section | Notes>

Each numbering family is off by default, so a document is unnumbered until you opt in. The website-chrome options apply only to a `type=website` document.
</section>

<section #sec:appendix-data | Embedded Data>

<sub-section | General Usage>

`<data>` is an invisible store for assets the document embeds — images and datasets — declared once with an id and pulled in by `@id`. It keeps a whole document self-contained in a single file.

<code #code:data-basic>
<data>
<fig #fig:logo png>iVBORw0KGgo…base64…</fig>
</data>

Later, in the body:

<fig src="@fig:logo" | The project logo.>
</code>

<sub-section | Notes>

The data block renders nothing itself — it holds the bytes. Reference an embedded asset by setting `src` to its id behind the `@` sigil.
</section>

<section #sec:appendix-library | Reference Library>

<sub-section | General Usage>

`<library>` is an invisible store for bibliographic references, pasted straight from a reference manager. Citations resolve against it, and `<bibliography>` prints the list.

<code #code:library-basic>
<library format=bibtex>
@book{strang2016, author={Gilbert Strang}, title={Introduction to Linear Algebra}, year={2016}}
@article{goodall2024, author={Jane Goodall}, title={Field Notes}, year={2024}}
</library>
</code>

<sub-section | Notes>

Cite an entry by its key — `<cite strang2016>` — anywhere in the body. `format` may be `bibtex` or another supported form, and `src` loads the entries from an external file instead of inline.
</section>
